# Oliver thesis → PLOS report writing style

**Primary voice reference:** [`Master Thesis.txt`](../../Master Thesis.txt) / `Master Thesis.pdf` (DTU MSc, Feb 2026).  
**Target manuscript:** [`docs/paper/plos_my_paper.tex`](../../docs/paper/plos_my_paper.tex).  
**LaTeX mechanics:** [`plos-compbiol-tex.mdc`](plos-compbiol-tex.mdc).  
**Structure pacing (not voice):** [`report-writing-qslp-style.mdc`](report-writing-qslp-style.mdc) — borrow QSLP *discipline* (numbers first, figure-first Methods); do **not** copy Ciudad's human-alignment framing.

When editing prose, **this rule governs voice**. QSLP rule governs section templates and word budget only.

---

## Voice fingerprint (from thesis analysis)

### What defines your writing

| Trait | Thesis pattern | Keep in PLOS report |
|-------|----------------|---------------------|
| **Register** | Formal British academic; impersonal in Methods/Results | Yes — `characterise`, `behaviour`, `organisation`, `generalise`, `prioritise` |
| **Argument shape** | Problem → limitation of baselines → principled extension → empirical ladder | Yes — compress to 1–2 sentences per step, not full subsections |
| **Validation ladder** | Synthetic → time-domain fail → features → cGMVAE → substages | Paper ladder: **HMM raw → spectral CNN → cGMVAE → cHMM → K-sweep** (thesis LOSO ~0.70 is contrast only, not headline) |
| **Scope language** | Subject-level vs population-level; train vs predictive NMI | Paper: **joint vs within-lab**; **incohort vs holdout**; always name the evaluation scope in the first sentence of a result paragraph |
| **Metrics** | NMI primary; log-likelihood secondary; mean ± SEM for reliability | Paper: **prior NMI** primary; best-of-3 seeds; macro accuracy/confusion as complements — cite SI for SEM tables |
| **Interpretation** | Claim → number → mechanistic reading → caveat | Yes — this is your strongest habit; keep it |
| **Mechanistic subsections** | Named blocks like *Why Time-Domain Inputs Break on MSSV* | PLOS: fold into Results as **2–3 sentences** after the table claim, not a titled subsubsection |
| **Hedging** | `suggests that`, `consistent with`, `which indicates`, `to the best of current knowledge` | Keep measured hedging for causality; drop empty hedges (`it is important to note`) |
| **Prior work** | Long Related Work with paradigm contrasts (supervised vs unsupervised vs HMM extensions) | Intro: **one sentence per competitor** + explicit delta (decoder-only, zero-shot holdout) |
| **Signposting** | Heavy forward/back refs (`see Section 5.1`, `which motivates Section 6`) | Replace with `\nameref{}` / Fig/Table cite **once**; cut roadmap paragraphs |

### Thesis phrases to preserve (your identity)

- *principled route*, *population-level transfer*, *cross-subject generalisation*, *representation mismatch*, *stage-discriminative*, *bout structure*, *latent structure*, *subject-conditioned*, *unsupervised alignment*, *residual covariate shift*, *heterogeneous recordings*, *interpretable substages*, *boundary-like refinements*, *macro-stage organisation*
- Contrast pairs you use naturally: **time-domain vs feature/spectral**; **static vs temporal prior**; **within-subject vs population**; **training vs predictive/holdout**; **HMM vs MAR-HMM / cGMVAE vs cHMM**

### Thesis bloat to cut for PLOS (small improvements)

| Thesis habit | PLOS fix |
|--------------|----------|
| Opening paragraph listing what the section will cover | Delete — start with the main result or protocol |
| 8–12 sentence paragraphs | **3–6 sentences**; one idea per paragraph |
| Repeated metric definitions | Define NMI once in Methods; Results cite Eq/Table only |
| `As shown in Table X, the results confirm…` (double filler) | `Table~\ref{…} shows…` or `In Table~\ref{…}, …` |
| Passive stack: *It was found that…* | Direct: *cHMM–GMVAE exceeds cGMVAE on every fold* |
| `to the best of our knowledge` every paragraph | Once in Intro for novelty claim only |
| Separate Discussion section repeating Results | Single **Results and discussion** — interpret inline |
| Thesis chapter numbers in prose | `\nameref{S…}` or `(Appendix)` |
| Log-likelihood in every sentence | Mention when explaining checkpointing or K-sweep dual axis only |

