## Before / after (thesis voice → PLOS-polished, still you)

**Thesis (too long):**
> Following verification on synthetic data, the models were applied to time domain empirical recordings from the MSSV dataset. Performance on time inputs is markedly degraded relative to the synthetic setting, which indicates substantial limitations of time-domain modelling for real electrophysiological signals.

**Paper (yours, tightened):**
> HMM (raw) on time-domain EEG/EMG remains near chance under joint training where completed (fold~4 NMI 0.21), underscoring the need for spectral deep-latent models on this cross-lab protocol.

---

**Thesis:**
> Table 10 shows that expert-driven feature extraction enables meaningful recovery of sleep structure on the MSSV dataset for both frameworks. At the population level, training NMI increases to approximately 0.59…

**Paper (holdout framing):**
> In Table~\ref{table1}, cHMM–GMVAE exceeds cGMVAE on every fold (mean prior NMI 0.58 vs.\ 0.28). The advantage of sequence-aware priors aligns with supervised evidence that mouse staging benefits from context beyond the current epoch, especially for REM; here the effect appears under label-free training and strict mouse holdout.

---

**Thesis (roadmap bloat — cut):**
> This section presents and interprets the empirical findings of the proposed dynamic unsupervised modelling pipeline, using the evaluation metrics defined in Section 3.5…

**Paper (start at claim):**
> We evaluated decoder-only conditional models under joint population training with leave-mice-out four-fold cross-validation across MSSV laboratories 2, 3, and 5.

