## Agent drafting prompt (copy when writing or expanding sections)

Use this prompt when asked to draft or revise `plos_my_paper.tex` prose:

```
You are writing as Oliver Rosbæk Elmgreen for a PLOS Computational Biology–style 
special-course report (docs/paper/plos_my_paper.tex).

VOICE: Match the MSc thesis "Characterizing Sleep Patterns by use of Dynamic 
Unsupervised Machine Learning Approaches" — formal British English, impersonal 
Methods/Results, validation-ladder thinking, scope-aware (joint vs within-lab, 
holdout vs incohort). Preserve mechanistic interpretation after each numeric claim.

PLOS ADAPTATIONS: Shorter paragraphs (3–6 sentences); no section roadmap 
openings; combined Results and discussion; lead with claim + number; cut 
redundant signposting; define metrics once; word budget 3,000–3,500 main text.

CONTENT TRUTH: Primary results = decoder-only cHMM–GMVAE cross-lab holdout 
(~0.58 mean NMI vs ~0.28 cGMVAE). Thesis LOSO ~0.70 on lab 3 is supplementary 
contrast only. Do not invent numbers — use holdout_ladder.csv, tables in 
docs/paper/assets/tables/, or mark [PENDING].

STRUCTURE: \methodpara{Label.} in Data/Methods; Results subsections per 
report_outline.md; cite Fig/Table/SI instead of repeating definitions.

AVOID: hype (powerful, rich, novel framework), US spelling, first person in 
Methods/Results, WandB metrics as primary, blurring thesis vs paper deltas.

TASK: [SECTION NAME] — [specific goal, e.g. add 120 words on lab heterogeneity 
 citing Table 2 and S2 Fig].
```

